   "note": "Nourai's root box reproduces Pokorny exactly: IEW p.301 heads the lemma '2. eis-, ei-n- ,Eis, Frost'' and lists 'Av. isu- ,eisig', aexa- n. ,Kälte', pämir. iš ,Kälte', osset. yex, ix ,Eis', afghan. asai ,Frost'; anord. iss … ags. is n., as. ahd. mhd. is n. ,Eis'; bsl. *inia- ,Reif''. The label 'Indo-European', the numbering 'Eis 2' (Pokorny's second eis-) and the gloss 'ice' are therefore all correct; Nourai's 'Eis' is simply Pokorny's German headword taken over as a root name. Modern reconstruction writes the root with the laryngeal, *h₁eyH- (Wiktionary, Proto-Iranian *Háyxam < PIE *h₁eyg-/*h₁eyH-), and the older handbooks are cautious about whether the Iranian and Germanic words really belong together: Walde-Pokorny I 108 says only 'Möglicherweise verwandt mit av. aēxa- \"Frost, Eis\", isu \"kalt\"', Buck 1.77 writes 'prob. : Av. aēxa- … NPers. yax … root connection?', and Etymonline still calls the Germanic word 'of uncertain origin'. That caution concerns the depth of the reconstruction, not the Iranian-Germanic equation itself, which every source on the page repeats. One purely graphic point: the printed page also carries an EMPTY box to the right of the Old Persian box, joined to it by an arrow; it is blank in the book (no language, no word), and the JSON extraction simply omits it.",
